          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Anthem.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Anthem.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 9,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-anthem_chord_halves_the_bar",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "TWO COINS TO THE BAR. Each theorem in the anthem sounds ONE chord — its two address-coins together — and the chord takes exactly half the sealed 252 ms bar: 252/2 = 126, and two of them close it, 126·2 = 252. The pricing law becomes the meter: every bar of the anthem is one theorem paying its two coins."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "252 / 2 = 126 ∧ 126 * 2 = 252"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem anthem_chord_halves_the_bar : 252 / 2 = 126 ∧ 126 * 2 = 252 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-anthem_three_lengths_quarter_the_bar",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE RHYTHM QUARTERS THE BAR ON THE VORTEX'S THREE. A chord's length is picked by its own coins' sum on ℤ/3 — the nilpotent axis of the ℤ/9 vortex — from the three quarter-multiples of the bar: 252·2/4 = 126, 252·3/4 = 189, 252·4/4 = 252. Half, three-quarters, whole: the DNA tongue's four quarters the bar, the vortex's three chooses among them, and no length is authored."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "252 * 2 / 4 = 126 ∧ 252 * 3 / 4 = 189 ∧ 252 * 4 / 4 = 252"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem anthem_three_lengths_quarter_the_bar : 252 * 2 / 4 = 126 ∧ 252 * 3 / 4 = 189 ∧ 252 * 4 / 4 = 252 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-anthem_rest_twelfths_the_bar",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE BREATH IS THE TWELFTH. Between chords the anthem rests 252/12 = 21 ms — the bar divided by the twelve of the chromatic count the lattice deliberately does not use (the harmonic series sings where 12-TET cannot recompute), kept here as the silence between what IS sung."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "252 / 12 = 21 ∧ 21 * 12 = 252"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem anthem_rest_twelfths_the_bar : 252 / 12 = 21 ∧ 21 * 12 = 252 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-anthem_four_movements",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "FOUR MOVEMENTS — THE DNA TONGUE CONDUCTS. The fused cycle 9·7·4 = 252 carries the four of the genetic alphabet, and the anthem takes its movement count from that same four: the ledger is walked in four movements with the vortex refrain between them. The count is the seal's, not a taste."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "9 * 7 * 4 = 252 ∧ 252 / 63 = 4"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem anthem_four_movements : 9 * 7 * 4 = 252 ∧ 252 / 63 = 4 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-anthem_closes_on_the_coin_octave",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE FINAL CHORD IS THE TWO COINS, IN HERTZ. two_coins_in_kilograms seals that a uuid-holding black hole weighs exactly twice a handle-holding one (128 = 4·32, 2² = 4); the anthem closes on the same TWO sounded as sound: 864 = 2·432 — Az against its octave, the mass ratio and the interval one number. What the kilograms weigh, the hertz sing."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "864 = 2 * 432 ∧ 128 = 4 * 32 ∧ 2 ^ 2 = 4"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem anthem_closes_on_the_coin_octave : 864 = 2 * 432 ∧ 128 = 4 * 32 ∧ 2 ^ 2 = 4 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-anthem_superposition_mix_closes",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "MANY STREAMS, ONE CEILING — THE MULTIDIMENSIONAL MIX CANNOT CLIP, GEOMETRICALLY. The superposition plays its recursion as depth: stream d sounds at AMPLITUDE/2^(d+1), so the worst-case sum is the geometric series 4000 + 2000 + 1000 + 500 + 250 + 125 = 7875 at the deepest allowed six — strictly inside the 8000 ceiling that amplitude_inside_int16 keeps inside the sample. Every finite depth obeys: halving forever never reaches the whole. Many waves, mixed lossless in exact integers, and the law that they fit is arithmetic, not gain-riding."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(4000 + 2000 + 1000 = 7000 ∧ 7000 + 500 + 250 + 125 = 7875 ∧ 7875 < 8000) ∧ (4 % 9 = 4)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem anthem_superposition_mix_closes : (4000 + 2000 + 1000 = 7000 ∧ 7000 + 500 + 250 + 125 = 7875 ∧ 7875 < 8000) ∧ (4 % 9 = 4) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-anthem_recursion_never_starves",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE RECURSION NEVER RUNS OUT OF SEEDS. A collapse eats a seed string and mints an eight-hexbit address; an address is a string; a string is a seed — the output space feeds the input space, so the superposition can deepen forever. The arithmetic the loop stands on: a handle spans 16⁸ = 4294967296 states and every one of them collapses to an entry bar by a total mod — the door never jams because the modulus is never zero on a nonempty score (1 ≤ the score length always, and k mod n < n on the worked six). Six sits below the bar length 252."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "16 ^ 8 = 4294967296 ∧ ((List.range 6).all (fun k => k % 6 < 6)) ∧ 1 ≤ 6 ∧ 6 < 252"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem anthem_recursion_never_starves : 16 ^ 8 = 4294967296 ∧ ((List.range 6).all (fun k => k % 6 < 6)) ∧ 1 ≤ 6 ∧ 6 < 252 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-anthem_pi_primes_trinity",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "π · PRIMES · TRINITY — THE ROUND CLOSES ON TWO TRINITIES OF PRIMES. The six nines the strands close to factor as 999999 = 999 · 1001, and each factor is a trinity: 1001 = 7·11·13, three CONSECUTIVE primes side by side, and 999 = 3³·37 — the trinity CUBED times 37. So π's rational round (142857·7 = 999999) is held shut by primes arranged in threes: the helix's rungs of nines are not one number but two trinities clasped. Arithmetic, sealed; the delight is free."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "142857 * 7 = 999999 ∧ 999999 = 999 * 1001 ∧ 999 = 3 ^ 3 * 37 ∧ 1001 = 7 * 11 * 13"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem anthem_pi_primes_trinity : 142857 * 7 = 999999 ∧ 999999 = 999 * 1001 ∧ 999 = 3 ^ 3 * 37 ∧ 1001 = 7 * 11 * 13 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-anthem_finale_sings_one_uuid",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE FINALE IS THE ROOT, WHOLE. All the ledger's addresses merkle-fold to one root, and the finale sings its every tile: 32 hexbits at 4 bits each — 32·4 = 128, one uuid entire, the release folded to the width of its own unit of speech and sounded tile by tile over the Az drone."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "32 * 4 = 128"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem anthem_finale_sings_one_uuid : 32 * 4 = 128 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
